WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.529
But everywhere, the natural world is
bursting with backstories. The world is

00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:11.010
made of—or more precisely, by—time. This is what I mean by timefulness: an

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.299
awareness of the rich natural history
that envelops us, a sense that we too live

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:20.100
in geologic time and are part of a
continuum from the planet's past into its

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.480
future. Timefulness is a deliberate
counterpoint to timelessness—an

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.660
unattainable, sterile, and, I will argue,
even dangerous idea. But we live in a

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.620
time-illiterate society, as I don't need to tell this audience. We would be

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.790
shocked if an educated adult had no
idea where the continents were on a

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:43.110
global map, but don't seem bothered by the fact that few average people have a

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:47.430
good sense of the geography of the
planet's past, even though we will spend

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:51.660
our entire lives here. We behave like bad tourists on earth, enjoying its amenities

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.410
and ransacking its bounty, without ever having noticed that it has its own

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.390
ancient language and customs. Many of the environmental problems we face have

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.290
arisen because we collectively lack a sense for the duration of chapters in

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:09.180
Earth's history, the rates of geologic
processes like groundwater flow, or the

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.360
intrinsic timescales of natural
phenomena like climate change.

